A shared key dictionary must enumerate what it HOLDS, not the first N keys of its key set.

_values has one slot per key the shared key set was built with and only _count of them are
occupied. countByEnumeratingWithState walked it as if slot index equalled entry index: it emitted
keyAtIndex: for slots 0 to _count whether or not the slot held anything, so it NAMED EMPTY SLOTS
and DROPPED every key stored past _count. getObjects:andKeys:count: in the same file already
carries a comment about exactly this defect, fixed there and not here; keyEnumerator had the
mirror image of it, answering every key in the key set including the unoccupied ones.

The side dictionary indexing was wrong too, subtracting sideCount from a cursor that counts from
_count.

NOT THEORETICAL. Measured with CIDER_TRACE_SHAREDKEYS, added here: iA Writer builds 67 shared key
dictionaries in one launch, 57 of them with 33 slots. A for-in over one of those listed the first
eleven key NAMES of the key set rather than the eleven entries it held.

This does NOT change what iA Writer draws. The library item attribute store that answers nil for
excerptProvider is a plain __NSCFDictionary, measured again after this change: still 56 misses on a
dictionary of 11. This is a separate correctness fix in a path that a roster application exercises
heavily, not a fix for #194.
